"The English word calligraphy comes from Greek, and dictionaries trace it to words for beauty and writing. People in many parts of the world have turned writing into an art. In this lesson we compare two traditions: writing Chinese characters with a brush, and writing the Latin alphabet with a broad-edge pen. Start with the brush. Its hairs are soft and springy. Press down, and the hairs spread out and the line grows thick. Lift up, and the line grows thin. A calligrapher can change the width anywhere in a stroke, in any direction. You practiced this in Lesson 2. Now the broad-edge pen. For more than a thousand years in Europe, the main writing tool was a quill, cut from a feather. Scribes cut the tip flat, like a tiny chisel. The writer holds this flat edge at one angle and keeps it there. Pressing harder does almost nothing, because the tip is stiff. So where do the thick and thin lines come from? From direction. When the pen moves across its own edge, it leaves a wide line. When it slides along its edge, it leaves a line as thin as a hair. In a round letter like o, the line swells and thins by itself as the pen goes around. The writing moves differently too. Traditional Chinese texts are written in columns from top to bottom, and the columns go from right to left. The Latin alphabet is written in rows from left to right. Chinese is built from thousands of characters, each with its own strokes; English uses twenty-six letters again and again. Both traditions ask for the same things: steady hands, patient practice, and a good eye for spacing. In this lesson, watch the two tools write side by side, turn the nib, and then try both yourself.": "c31a14ef8bf8",
